  <div class="fstep"><strong>July 2026.</strong> Charges finally framed on 13 July; a week later, the High Court rejects bail — citing seizures, witness statements, threat to State sovereignty, undue delay attributable to the appellant and his supposed late surrender.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>November 2026.</strong> The Supreme Court grants leave, corrects the surrender finding from the custody certificate, applies K.A. Najeeb, and orders release on bail subject to trial-court conditions, encouraging the Sessions Court to expedite.</div>
</div>

<h2>The legal spine: Najeeb inside Section 43D(5)</h2>
<p>Section 43D(5) UAPA bars bail where the chargesheet material makes the accusations prima facie true. But as the Court reiterated, the embargo does not extinguish a constitutional court&rsquo;s power to grant bail where fundamental rights are violated — above all by unreasonably long pre-trial custody. <em>Union of India v. K.A. Najeeb</em>, (2021) 3 SCC 713, remains the controlling authority. The judgment applies it with arithmetic candour:</p>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Metric</th><th>Value</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Custody served</td><td>Over four years (arrest July 2022)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Committal to charge-framing</td><td>More than three years — 18 months of it after the appellant&rsquo;s surrender, unexplained</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Witnesses examined</td><td>None of 39</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Co-accused position</td><td>On bail since January 2024</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The custody-certificate lesson</h2>
<p>The decisive forensic move was documentary: against the State&rsquo;s narrative of a two-and-a-half-month abscondence after temporary bail, the defence pointed to the prison&rsquo;s own custody certificate showing timely surrender. A factual premise of the rejection collapsed, and with it the attribution of trial delay to the accused. For practitioners, the point generalises — nominal rolls, custody certificates and jail records are official documents that outrank assertions in status reports, and they should be obtained and placed on record in every delay-based bail plea.</p>

<h2>Practice pointers</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Build the delay ledger.</strong> Date of arrest, committal, charge, each adjournment and its author — a one-page chronology makes the Najeeb argument concrete.</p>
  <p><strong>Answer &ldquo;delay attributable to accused&rdquo; with records.</strong> Where the State blames the accused, demand the documentary basis; prison and court records frequently contradict it.</p>
  <p><strong>Plead the innocent-explanation context.</strong> Professional duty, residence or occupation that explains presence or contact is legitimate bail-stage material even where the merits are barred from scrutiny.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This note summarises a judgment of the Supreme Court of India for general information. It is not legal advice; bail in UAPA matters depends on the material and custody record in each case.</div>
